{
  "request_id": "e86cc20add59491c268fcd38",
  "rewritten_text": "I thought this charming lie was told with simple beauty, though I had only heard it once, which makes a difference; it surely delighted the others when it was new. Sir Dinadan the Humorist woke first and roused the group with a crude prank. He tied metal mugs to a dog's tail and released it, sending the panicked animal racing around while other dogs chased it, crashing into everything and creating a deafening, chaotic mess. The crowd laughed until they wept, some falling from their chairs to roll on the floor in delight. They were just like children. Sir Dinadan was so proud that he recounted the origin of his brilliant idea until everyone was exhausted, still laughing long after the others had stopped. He then decided to give a humorous speech, which consisted of the most tired, worn-out jokes I had ever heard. He was worse than any circus clown. It was depressing to sit there, thirteen hundred years before my own time, listening to stale, ancient jokes that had annoyed me as a boy in the future. It convinced me that no new joke is possible. Everyone laughed at these relics, as they always do. The boy, however, did not laugh; he scoffed at everything. He claimed most of Dinadan’s jokes were rotten and the rest were petrified. I agreed, noting that these jokes could only be classified by geologic eras. That comment went over the boy's head, as geology had not been invented yet, but I made a note of it, intending to educate the public later. There is no point in wasting a good idea just because the time isn't right. Then, Sir Kay stood up and began his own performance, using me as his target."
}

