  "request_id": "0134bfe5746c864ca69f0170",
  "rewritten_text": "I question how long you might have persisted had you been left alone, or when you would have spoken had I not prompted you. My decision to thank you for your assistance with Lydia clearly had a significant impact. Perhaps too much, for what becomes of the moral if our comfort arises from a broken promise? I should not have broached the topic. This is quite improper. You need not worry, for the moral remains sound. Lady Catherine’s unwarranted attempts to divide us actually resolved my uncertainties. My current happiness does not stem from your desire to express gratitude; I was not inclined to wait for your lead. My aunt’s news gave me hope, and I was resolved to learn the truth immediately. Lady Catherine has been immensely useful, which should please her, as she enjoys being helpful. But tell me, why did you come to Netherfield? Was it simply to ride to Longbourn and feel awkward, or did you intend something more significant? My true goal was to see you and determine if I might ever hope for your affection. My stated purpose, or at least what I told myself, was to see if your sister still cared for Bingley, and if so, to make the confession to him that I have since made. Will you ever have the nerve to tell Lady Catherine what is to happen? I am more likely to lack time than courage, Elizabeth. Still, it must be done, and if you provide paper, I shall do it at once. If I did not have a letter of my own to write, I might sit beside you and admire your steady penmanship, as another lady once did. However, I have an aunt who must not be ignored any longer. Because she was reluctant to admit how much her closeness with Mr. Darcy had been exaggerated, Elizabeth had not yet replied to Mrs. Gardiner’s lengthy letter. Now, however, having news she knew would be most welcome, she felt ashamed that her aunt and uncle had already missed three days of joy and immediately began to write: I should have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, for your long and kind account, but I was too annoyed to write. You assumed more than was true. But now, you may assume whatever you wish; let your imagination run wild, and unless you believe me already married, you cannot be far from the truth."
}

