  "request_id": "b2939fc365e718c81139210d",
  "rewritten_text": "Tom joined the Cadets of Temperance, drawn by their flashy uniforms. He pledged to avoid smoking, chewing tobacco, and swearing while a member. He quickly discovered that promising to abstain only made him crave those very things. His urge to drink and curse became so intense that only the prospect of wearing his red sash kept him in the group. He looked forward to a grand public funeral for the dying Judge Frazer, but the Judge kept recovering. Tom grew frustrated and resigned, only for the Judge to die that same night. Tom felt betrayed by the man's inconsistency. The funeral was impressive, and the Cadets marched in a way that would have made Tom jealous, but he was relieved to be free. Surprisingly, once he was allowed to drink and swear again, he lost all interest in doing so. Soon, Tom found his newfound freedom boring. He tried keeping a diary, but gave up after three days of inactivity. A traveling minstrel show arrived, and Tom and Joe Harper organized their own troupe, enjoying it for two days. Even the Fourth of July was a letdown; it rained, canceling the parade, and the visiting U.S. Senator, Mr. Benton, was a massive disappointment because he was nowhere near the twenty-five feet tall Tom had imagined. Then, a circus came to town."
}

