  "request_id": "93bdf56402009516edef3f27",
  "rewritten_text": "He would never have escaped without my help. Morgan le Fay hated him deeply and would never have shown him mercy. His crime was mostly thoughtless, though; he had simply mentioned her red hair. While true, it was a social blunder, as people of her status are said to have auburn hair. Consider that among these forty-seven prisoners, five had been forgotten entirely. These elderly, frail people could no longer recall their own names or why they were imprisoned. The priests who visited them only taught them to accept their suffering as God's will, and even their records were lost. We only knew they had been locked away for at least thirty-five years. The king and queen viewed them as mere inherited property, like old furniture, and never thought to release them. When I asked the queen why they remained, she was puzzled; the idea of freeing them had never occurred to her. To her, they were simply assets, and people rarely discard property even when it lacks value. I led these human ghosts out into the sunlight, having blindfolded them first to protect their sensitive eyes. They were a tragic sight—skeletal, withered figures who stood as the true legacy of the monarchy and the church."
}

