  "request_id": "640c983061212872d4430e97",
  "rewritten_text": "He would never have escaped without my intervention. Morgan le Fay despised him completely and would never have shown mercy, even though his offense was born of thoughtlessness rather than malice. He had merely mentioned her red hair; while true, it was a social blunder, as such hair is properly called auburn among the elite. Consider that among the forty-seven captives, five had lost all record of their names, crimes, and arrival dates. These elderly, broken individuals had forgotten their own histories. The priests tasked with teaching them that God ordained their suffering for some divine purpose held only vague traditions about them, confirming only that they had been imprisoned for at least thirty-five years. The king and queen viewed these people as mere heirlooms, inherited assets with no known history, and thus felt no interest in them. When I asked the queen why she had not released them, she was baffled; the thought had never occurred to her. It became clear that, given her upbringing, she viewed these prisoners simply as property. We rarely discard property, even when it holds no value. When I finally led these human bats into the afternoon sun—having blindfolded them first to protect their long-darkened eyes—they were a harrowing sight. They were skeletal, wretched figures, the true products of a monarchy sanctioned by the church."
}

