  "rewritten_text": "Tom joined the Cadets of Temperance, drawn by their flashy uniforms. He pledged to quit smoking, chewing tobacco, and swearing while a member. He soon discovered that promising not to do something only made him crave it more. His urge to drink and curse became so strong that only the prospect of wearing his red sash kept him in the group. He hoped to show off at the upcoming Fourth of July parade, but he quit the order after only two days. Instead, he pinned his hopes on Judge Frazer, a local official who was dying. Tom expected a grand public funeral and spent three days anxiously checking on the Judge’s health. He even practiced wearing his uniform in front of the mirror. However, the Judge kept recovering, which annoyed Tom. He finally quit the group, only for the Judge to die that very night. Tom felt betrayed and vowed never to trust the man again. The funeral was impressive, and the Cadets marched in a way that would have made Tom jealous. Still, he was free again. Surprisingly, once he was allowed to drink and swear, he no longer wanted to. The thrill was gone. Soon, Tom found his newfound freedom boring. He tried keeping a diary, but gave up after three days of nothing to report. When a minstrel show arrived, Tom and Joe Harper formed their own troupe and enjoyed two days of fun. Even the Fourth of July was a letdown; it rained, canceling the parade, and the guest of honor, a United States Senator, was a disappointment because he was not the giant Tom had imagined. Then, a circus came to town."
}

