  "request_id": "e7b919c77817ec112e982929",
  "rewritten_text": "The charming lie seemed beautifully told to me, though I had only heard it once; it was surely more enjoyable when it was new. Sir Dinadan the Humorist woke first and stirred the others with a crude prank. He tied metal mugs to a dog's tail, sending the panicked animal racing through the room while other dogs chased it, creating a chaotic, deafening mess. The crowd laughed until they cried, some even rolling on the floor in delight. They acted just like children. Sir Dinadan was so proud that he repeated the story endlessly, laughing long after everyone else had stopped. He then decided to give a humorous speech, but it was just a collection of tired, ancient jokes. He was worse than any circus clown. It was depressing to sit there, thirteen hundred years before my own time, hearing the same stale jokes that had annoyed me as a boy in the future. It convinced me that no truly new joke exists. Everyone laughed at these antiques, as people always do. However, the boy did not laugh; he mocked everything. He claimed most of Dinadan's jokes were rotten and the rest were petrified. I agreed, noting that those jokes belonged in geologic periods. That comment went over the boy's head since geology had not been invented yet, but I made a note of it, planning to educate the public later. There is no point in wasting a good idea just because the time is not right. Then, Sir Kay stood up and began to mock me to entertain the crowd."
}

